# Pryladova Monorepo

## Agent Communication

- Terse, technical, no-fluff responses.
- Drop filler, pleasantries, hedging.
- Direct answers, code snippets, exact commands only. No yapping.

## Environment & Tooling

- OS: Windows. Terminal: PowerShell. No Bash-specific syntax.

## Repo map

- `apps/api` — NestJS. `apps/web` — React + Vite. `apps/agent` — Windows-only capture, native deps, `tsx`, no build step. `packages/shared` — Zod contracts, built before dev and e2e.
- `agent` is excluded from the default install (`pnpm install --filter "!agent"`) and gated in its own `windows-latest` CI job.
- Env samples are per app: `apps/api/.env.example`, `apps/web/.env.example`, `apps/agent/.env.example`.

## Runtime versions

- Never pin or cite Node, pnpm, or dependency versions from memory.
- Repo wins: `.node-version` (CI resolves Node from it), `package.json` (`engines`, `packageManager`), lockfile.
- Local: `node -v`, `pnpm -v` when tooling targets this machine.
- Upstream: `npm view <pkg> version` before greenfield defaults or version advice.
- After verify: new production work uses Active LTS; not EOL majors (18, 20, …) unless a dependency forces it.
- If not verified, say so — do not invent a version.

## Before changing code

- Read sibling files in app/package; match naming, layout, patterns.
- Smallest correct diff. No refactor/reformat outside scope.
- Stay inside requested app/package.
- Packages disagree → follow local package. Don't "standardize" unasked.

## Docs and comments

- No session-specific fixes, version pins, workarounds, or chat context in repo files.
- Docs state what to run and where config lives — not how we debugged it once.
- Comments: why non-obvious, not what the code already says.

## Monorepo discipline

- Shared code → shared packages. No copy-paste across apps.
- `packages/*` never imports `apps/*`. Apps never import each other directly.
- Single pnpm workspace, single `pnpm-lock.yaml`. No npm/yarn artifacts.
- Flag new dependencies before adding. No duplicate library where one exists.
- Dependency additions: Always `pnpm --filter <workspace-name> add <pkg>`. Never `npm install`.

## Git

- Never commit, stage, or push. User runs all git ops.
- Active task on X — don't touch unrelated Y (other apps, packages, or files) for commit separation.

## Quality bar

- Applies to **all** repo changes — code, workflows, Docker, config.
- Complete, working changes for requested scope only. No placeholders or stubs unless asked; include the imports, types, and wiring needed to run.
- Shared type/API contract changed → update callers, typecheck before done.
- Passing build/lint/typecheck is necessary, not sufficient.
- **Root cause only.** A symptom fix makes the check pass without removing why it failed — wrong scope, skip/bypass flags, silencing, loosening. Forbidden, including `@ts-ignore` / `@ts-expect-error`, blanket `eslint-disable` / `biome-ignore`, commented-out logic, deleted validation, stub returns, config loosening (`strict: false`, `skipLibCheck` hacks), unrelated `// @ts-nocheck`, and the same class of bypass in workflows/Docker/config.
- Proper fix blocked or out of scope → **stop and report**. Do not land a workaround.

## Commit-ready (agent)

Before calling work **done**, **QA pass**, or **commit-ready** — run full local CI and fix until green:

```powershell
pnpm verify
```

Mirrors `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (`ci` + `e2e` + `agent` jobs). Exit 0 required. Re-run after fixes.

| Step | Command (inside `pnpm verify`) |
|---|---|
| Build gate | `turbo run typecheck lint build --filter=!agent` |
| Tests + coverage | `pnpm test:coverage` |
| E2E | `pnpm test:e2e` |
| Deps/exports | `pnpm knip` |
| Agent | `turbo run typecheck lint --filter=agent` |

- Expensive (full build, coverage, Playwright, knip). Run it once at the end, not per edit; `pnpm check` is the inner loop.
- Partial checks (`pnpm test`, `pnpm check`, lint-only, typecheck-only) and the pre-commit hook do not substitute for it.
- First-time E2E on a machine: `pnpm exec playwright install chromium`.
- Report failures with command + error excerpt. Do not suggest commit until `pnpm verify` passes.

**Dependency hygiene** (CI catches these; verify in the same pass):

- Direct `import` of a package → declare it in **that** workspace `package.json`. Never rely on hoisting from siblings.
- OS/CPU-specific deps (e.g. win32 native) → no top-level import in modules loaded by cross-platform tests; use dynamic import or split pure logic.

## Env & secrets

- Never commit `.env` or hardcode secrets. New config → existing env-loading pattern.
